# Unity C# Standards

## Check the project before writing code
- Read `ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt` and only use APIs available in that Unity version.
- Read `Packages/manifest.json` to know the render pipeline (Built-in / URP / HDRP), Input System (new vs legacy), and other packages.
  Write shaders, materials, and input code for what is actually installed. Do not assume.
- Match the style of surrounding code when it differs from this file, unless the user asks to change it.

## Naming and layout
- Types, methods, properties, public fields: `PascalCase`. Locals and parameters: `camelCase`.
- Private fields: `_camelCase`. Constants: `PascalCase`. Interfaces: `IName`.
- One top-level type per file; filename equals type name.
- Always declare access modifiers. Use file-scoped or block namespaces consistently with the existing code.
- Order inside classes: constants, serialized fields, private fields, properties, Unity messages
  (`Awake`, `OnEnable`, `Start`, `Update`, ...), public methods, private methods.

## Serialization
- Use `[SerializeField] private` instead of public fields for Inspector values.
- When renaming a serialized field, add `[FormerlySerializedAs("oldName")]` so existing scenes and prefabs keep their data.
- Never change the type of an existing serialized field without telling the user (data loss risk).
- Add `[RequireComponent]` when a component depends on another.

## Unity-specific correctness
- Never use `?.`, `??`, or `is null` on `UnityEngine.Object` types. Use `if (obj != null)` / `if (!obj)`.
- Prefer `TryGetComponent` over `GetComponent` + null check.
- Cache component and object references. No `Find*`, `GetComponent*`, or `Camera.main` inside `Update`/`FixedUpdate`.
- Avoid per-frame allocations (LINQ, `new` collections, string concatenation, boxing) in hot paths.
- Prefer events/callbacks over polling. Unsubscribe in `OnDisable`/`OnDestroy` what you subscribed in `OnEnable`/`Awake`.
- Do not introduce singletons or static state by default. Prefer serialized references or explicit dependencies. Ask if unsure.
- Use `ScriptableObject` for shared, designer-editable data instead of hardcoded values.
- Use `#if UNITY_EDITOR` only when unavoidable; otherwise keep editor code in `Assets/Editor/`.

## Quality
- Public APIs get a one-line `/// <summary>`. Do not comment the obvious.
- No magic numbers: use serialized fields or named constants.
- Logs: prefix with the class name, keep them minimal, and remove temporary debug logs before finishing.
- No dead code, no commented-out code, no TODOs left behind without mention in the summary.
- Keep methods small and focused. Split anything that grows beyond ~50 lines or does more than one job.
